.TH EMERALD.ALIAS 5 "2026-09-07" "@PROJECT_NAME@" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
@PROJECT_NAME@.alias \- package alias database
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I @SYSCONFDIR@/@PROJECT_NAME@.alias
maps a real package name to an installed alias name. Emerald uses aliases while resolving dependencies, allowing a compatible provider to satisfy dependencies on another package.
.SH FORMAT
Each non-empty, non-comment line contains two whitespace-separated fields:
.PP
.EX
REAL-PACKAGE ALIAS-PACKAGE
.EE
.PP
The first field is the real package (the dependency provider); the second is the installed alias. Lines beginning with \fB#\fR and blank lines are ignored.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
openssl libressl
.EE
.PP
This declares that \fBlibressl\fR is the installed alias/provider for dependencies referring to \fBopenssl\fR, subject to Emerald's dependency resolution rules.
.SH MANAGEMENT
Use \fB@PROJECT_NAME@ alias list\fR, \fB@PROJECT_NAME@ alias add REAL ALIAS\fR, and \fB@PROJECT_NAME@ alias remove REAL\fR rather than editing the file manually. Changes require root privileges.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR @PROJECT_NAME@ (1)
